WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429
When vibes stop working.

2
00:00:01.709 --> 00:00:03.395
Every team starts the same way.

3
00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:07.536
You write a prompt, try it on a few inputs, read the answers, and adjust.

4
00:00:07.816 --> 00:00:13.619
This works well at first, because early problems are obvious and one look tells you whether a change helped.

5
00:00:13.899 --> 00:00:20.512
Studio 1 had you do exactly this, plus a little more: the prompt test harness in week 2 was a small eval.

6
00:00:20.792 --> 00:00:22.115
Then it stops working.

7
00:00:22.395 --> 00:00:32.165
Hamel Husain describes this arc in his essay “Your AI Product Needs Evals,” using Rechat’s real estate assistant, Lucy, as the case study.

8
00:00:32.445 --> 00:00:35.645
Prompt engineering brought quick gains, then progress stalled.

9
00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:38.699
Fixing one failure exposed or caused others.

10
00:00:38.979 --> 00:00:45.485
Nobody could see how the system behaved across the full range of requests, and the prompt grew long and fragile.

11
00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:49.563
The team’s way out was to build evaluation into how they worked.

12
00:00:49.843 --> 00:00:51.357
The mechanism is simple.

13
00:00:51.637 --> 00:00:56.651
A product with a dozen behaviors to protect has too many interactions to hold in your head.

14
00:00:56.931 --> 00:01:03.672
Each change helps some inputs and hurts others, and spot-checking five examples samples almost none of the space.

15
00:01:03.952 --> 00:01:09.819
Without measurement you can’t tell progress from churn, and you tend to keep whichever version you looked at last.

16
00:01:10.099 --> 00:01:19.592
An eval replaces the spot check with a repeatable measurement: a fixed set of inputs, a way to grade each output, and a number you can compare across versions.

17
00:01:19.872 --> 00:01:25.824
This studio teaches you to build them, starting this week with the step most teams skip: looking at your data.
